  "title": "Chapter 13 – Midwifery & Obstetrics",
  "order": 13,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "91mio1m",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Obstetrics and midwifery encompass comprehensive maternal care from pre-conception through antenatal, intrapartum, postnatal, and essential newborn care.",
        "Pregnancy spans three distinct trimesters: First (1-12 weeks), Second (13-28 weeks), and Third (29-40 weeks).",
        "Signs of pregnancy are categorized into Presumptive (subjective), Probable (objective), and Positive (diagnostic) indicators.",
        "Labor progresses through four defined stages: Cervical dilation, Delivery of fetus, Placental expulsion, and the Postpartum recovery/observation phase.",
        "Active management of third stage of labor (AMTSL) and vigilant antenatal care prevent common complications such as PPH and pre-eclampsia."
      ],
      "id": "xsffhzr"
    },
